  "narrative": "All outcome_006 claims remain direction -1, so positive signed estimates mean feature_060=1 has stronger dependency, i.e. a more negative outcome_006 score, than feature_060=0. Using the absolute outcome_006 mean of about 0.175, the approximate 10% clinical threshold is 0.0175 score units. The central question is whether the accepted feature_024-high association is specifically concentrated when feature_034=1 or merely reflects broader subgroup/main-effect structure.\n\nH159 is the corrected direct interaction estimating whether the feature_060 contrast is stronger for feature_034=1 than feature_034=0 within feature_024 at or above its median. Its estimate is +0.0562, about 32% of the absolute outcome scale, but the interval (-0.0394, 0.1517) crosses zero and is wide. Because it is central to effect modification by feature_034 but imprecise, I keep it unresolved and active rather than accept it.\n\nH160 is the most compelling new positive contrast: the feature_060 simple effect within feature_024 at or above Q3 and feature_034=1 is +0.1136, about 65% of the absolute outcome scale, with adequate cells (128/116) and a discovery interval excluding zero (+0.0093, 0.2179). However, the lower bound is below the clinical threshold and the subgroup is nested after threshold selection and feature_034 splitting. This supports a dataset-specific intersection association but not yet causal or generalizable evidence. I keep H160 unresolved and active and request independent validation of H160 because it is the sharpest positive nested effect and the most likely claim to receive decisive independent evidence.\n\nH161 is the high-Q3 direct interaction: +0.0968, but the interval (-0.0458, 0.2395) is very imprecise. It does not establish feature_034 modification in the high-Q3 subgroup. I keep it unresolved but deferred because H160 validation and H159 are more informative first.\n\nThe complementary controls largely weaken a simple feature_034-only explanation of the accepted feature_024-high signal. H162, low-feature_024 feature_034 interaction, is +0.0136 and well below threshold with a wide interval crossing zero; I reject it for the hypothesized stronger-dependence direction. H163, the low-feature_024 feature_034=1 simple feature_060 contrast, is near null (+0.0052) and is rejected. H164, high-Q3 feature_034=0 simple contrast, is +0.0167, just below threshold and imprecise, so the high-Q3 feature_060 signal is not present among feature_034=0 and H164 is rejected.\n\nH165, baseline feature_034 within high feature_024, has signed estimate -0.0294, which is opposite the hypothesized positive direction and interval includes zero. It does not support the hypothesis that feature_034=1 is stronger-dependence in the high-feature_024 half; I reject and close it as a support for this line, though it raises the possibility of opposite baseline structure. H166, baseline feature_034 within low feature_024, is +0.0538 with interval (+0.0034, 0.1043), indicating a promising feature_034 main-effect subgroup association independent of feature_060. The lower bound is below the clinical threshold and it is an unvalidated newly discovered subgroup main effect, so I keep it unresolved and deferred rather than accept it.\n\nOutcome specificity remains favorable for outcome_006. H167, outcome_008 within high feature_024 and feature_034=1, is -0.0140 with a zero-crossing interval, opposite/small relative to its approximately 0.0282 threshold; reject. H168, outcome_013 in the same intersection, is +0.0302, slightly above its approximately 0.0269 threshold, but the interval crosses zero and the result is selected, so I do not accept it as evidence for broadened outcome sensitivity; reject and close.\n\nThe secondary outcome_006 binary main-marker controls give mixed signals. H22, feature_042=1 versus 0, is +0.0534 with interval (+0.0114, 0.0954), excluding zero and above the 0.0175 clinical threshold, but the lower bound is below threshold and this